= Cosmic inflation
{wiki}

Cosmic inflation is an early period of accelerated expansion, often modeled by a scalar field whose potential energy dominates its kinetic energy.

= Graceful exit from inflation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{title2=$\epsilon_H\to1$}

= Graceful exit
{synonym}

A <graceful exit from inflation> ends sustained <accelerating expansion> and connects it to a hot <radiation-dominated universe>. Crossing $\epsilon_H=1$ ends acceleration in a homogeneous expanding model; <reheating> requires an additional mechanism to transfer <inflaton> energy into other species. Pure <exponential-potential power-law inflation> keeps $\epsilon_H$ constant and its <inflaton potential> has no finite minimum, so neither an automatic endpoint nor oscillatory <reheating> follows from that potential alone.

= Power-law inflation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{title2=$a(t)\propto t^p,\quad p>1$}

<Power-law inflation> is <cosmic inflation> with a <scale factor> proportional to $t^p$ for constant $p>1$. Its <Hubble parameter> is $H=p/t$ and its <first Hubble slow-roll parameter> is $\epsilon_H=1/p$. The <comoving Hubble radius> decreases as $t^{1-p}$. A canonical <inflaton> with an exponential <inflaton potential> admits an exact example. Because $\epsilon_H$ is constant, an indefinitely maintained power law has no <graceful exit from inflation>.

= Exponential-potential power-law inflation
{parent=Power-law inflation}
{title2=$V=V_0e^{-c\phi/m_P},\quad a\propto t^{16\pi/c^2}$}

Use <natural units>, the unreduced <Planck mass> $m_P=G^{-1/2}$, $V_0>0$ and $c\ne0$. In a flat universe dominated by a canonical <inflaton>, $\phi=(2m_P/c)\log(\beta t)$ and $a\propto t^p$ solve the <inflaton equation of motion> and <Friedmann equation> exactly when $p=16\pi/c^2$ and $\beta^2=V_0c^2/[2m_P^2(3p-1)]$. The positive-potential scaling solution requires $p>1/3$, while <cosmic inflation> requires $p>1$. Its constant <equation-of-state parameter> is $w=-1+2/(3p)$. In terms of the <reduced Planck mass> $M=m_P/\sqrt{8\pi}$, the exponential slope is $\lambda=c/\sqrt{8\pi}$ and the same exponent is $p=2/\lambda^2$.

= F-term hybrid inflation
{c}
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{title2=$W=gS(uv-\zeta)$}

The bilinear hybrid <superpotential> couples an inflaton $S$ to two waterfall fields $u,v$. In global <supersymmetry>, $V=|g|^2(|uv-\zeta|^2+|S|^2(|u|^2+|v|^2))$. The branch $u=v=0$ breaks supersymmetry and is transversely stable for $|S|^2>|\zeta|$, while the zero-energy branch $S=0$, $uv=\zeta$ is supersymmetric. A canonical <Kähler potential> in <supergravity> slightly lifts the inflationary valley. It must not be described as two isolated stationary minima of the full potential.

= Supersymmetric vacuum manifold of a bilinear hybrid superpotential
{parent=F-term hybrid inflation}
{title2=$S=0,\quad uv=\zeta$}

For nonzero $g$ and $\zeta$, these field values give $W=0$ and all <Kähler covariant derivatives of a superpotential> zero, hence zero <supergravity auxiliary fields> and zero vacuum energy. With only the supplied chiral fields, the product condition gives a complex vacuum manifold rather than one isolated point. A specified gauged opposite-charge symmetry can add a <D-flatness> condition and a gauge quotient, but no such gauge sector should be invented from the field labels alone.

= Waterfall instability in F-term hybrid inflation
{parent=F-term hybrid inflation}
{title2=$m_\pm^2\simeq|g|^2(|S|^2\pm|\zeta|)$}

In the small-field global approximation, the waterfall fluctuations on $u=v=0$ have the displayed masses. One becomes negative when $|S|^2<|\zeta|$, driving the fields towards the <supersymmetric vacuum> branch. In the canonical supergravity model, the exact masses follow from expanding the exponential and Kähler-covariant derivatives; their Planck-suppressed corrections should not be discarded unless the corresponding field and symmetry-breaking scales are small.

= Canonical-Kähler correction to an F-term hybrid valley
{parent=F-term hybrid inflation}
{title2=$V(S,0,0)=|g\zeta|^2e^x(1-x+x^2),\quad x=|S|^2/M_p^2$}

The <supergravity F-term potential> on the inflationary branch has the displayed exact form. Its expansion is $|g\zeta|^2(1+x^2/2+O(x^3))$, so the inflaton quadratic mass cancels at tree level for this canonical model. For $x>0$ its derivative is $|g\zeta|^2e^xx(1+x)>0$; the branch is therefore a transverse valley, not a stationary supersymmetry-breaking vacuum.

= de Sitter approximation
{c}
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{title2=$a(\tau)\simeq-1/(H\tau)$}

Approximately constant positive <Hubble parameter> gives a nearly <de Sitter spacetime> expansion in flat slicing. Its scale factor can be approximated by $a=-1/(H\tau)$ with negative <conformal time> increasing toward zero. Slow variation of the expansion rate limits the accuracy of this approximation.

= Reheating
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

Reheating transfers the <inflaton> energy into other particles after <cosmic inflation>, eventually establishing a hot thermal bath. It requires interactions in addition to a background <scalar potential>. The duration and averaged <equation-of-state parameter> of reheating affect the <number of e-folds> between a chosen scale's horizon exit and the end of inflation.

= Reheating temperature
{parent=Reheating}
{title2=$T_R$}

The reheating temperature characterizes the thermal radiation bath after energy from a nonthermal component has been transferred and thermalized. For <sudden decay of a dominant nonrelativistic relic>, $T_R\simeq[3M_{\rm Pl}^2\Gamma^2/(c_2g_\star)]^{1/4}$. It differs from the residual radiation temperature immediately before decay.

= Quartic-inflation prompt-reheating estimate
{parent=Reheating temperature}
{title2=$T_R\sim\lambda^{1/4}g_*^{-1/4}M_P$}

For a quartic <inflaton> potential, an endpoint field of order the unreduced <Planck mass> leaves density of order $\lambda M_P^4$. If prompt, efficient <reheating> converts it to equilibrium radiation, $\rho=(\pi^2/30)g_*T_R^4$ gives the displayed scaling. With the potential-only endpoint estimate $\phi_e=M_P/\sqrt\pi$, the coefficient is $[15/(2\pi^4)]^{1/4}$. End-of-inflation <kinetic energy> changes that coefficient by order one, and delayed transfer or incomplete thermalization requires a reheating model rather than the potential alone.

= Sudden decay of a dominant nonrelativistic relic
{parent=Reheating}
{title2=$\rho_\phi\simeq3M_{\rm Pl}^2\Gamma^2$}

The sudden-decay approximation converts a dominant cold relic's rest-mass density into radiation at $H\simeq\Gamma$, neglecting expansion during the conversion. Its pre-decay radiation temperature obeys $T_-^3\simeq3M_{\rm Pl}^2\Gamma^2/(c_1g_\star Ym)$, while the <reheating temperature> is set by the post-decay radiation energy density. Constant relativistic counts and prompt thermalization are additional assumptions.

= Entropy production by decay of a dominant relic
{parent=Sudden decay of a dominant nonrelativistic relic}
{title2=$s_+/s_-=(1+\rho_\phi/\rho_r)^{3/4}$}

At fixed <scale factor> and unchanged relativistic counts, instantaneous conversion of a relic into thermal radiation gives the displayed entropy-density enhancement. It exceeds one because the gas receives energy from outside its prior equilibrium sector. This does not contradict <cosmological entropy conservation>, whose source-free reversible assumptions fail during decay and thermalization.

= Number of e-folds
{title2=$N=\log(a/a_i)$}
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

The number of e-folds during an interval of expansion is the logarithmic growth of the <scale factor>:
$$
N=\log\frac{a_f}{a_i}=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}H\,dt.
$$

= Primordial perturbation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

A primordial perturbation is an early-universe fluctuation that seeds later cosmic microwave background anisotropy and large-scale structure. Inflation generates scalar curvature perturbations and tensor perturbations from quantum fluctuations.

= Dimensionless cosmological power spectrum
{parent=Primordial perturbation}
{title2=$\Delta_X=k^3P_X/(2\pi^2)$}

= Dimensionless power spectrum
{synonym}

The dimensionless spectrum is the contribution to a statistically isotropic field variance per logarithmic <wavenumber> interval: $\langle X^2\rangle=\int d\log k\,\Delta_X(k)$, with any necessary smoothing or regulators. Some conventions denote this same quantity $\Delta_X^2$. The definition, not the superscript convention, fixes its meaning.

= Dimensional and dimensionless matter spectra across equality
{parent=Dimensionless cosmological power spectrum}
{title2=$\mathscr P(k)=k^3P(k)/(2\pi^2)$}

The dimensionless spectrum is $\mathscr P(k)=k^3P(k)/(2\pi^2)$. If it rises as $k^4$ below equality and as $\ln^2k$ above equality, then the dimensional spectrum rises as $k$ and falls as $k^{-3}\ln^2k$. Thus the dimensional spectrum has a turnover while the dimensionless spectrum only bends to a much slower rise. A schematic transition must smoothly join the two asymptotic regimes.

= Inflaton
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

A canonical homogeneous inflaton $\phi(t)$ has
$$
\rho=\frac12\dot\phi^2+V(\phi),
\qquad
P=\frac12\dot\phi^2-V(\phi),
$$
and obeys $\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0$.

= Inflaton potential
{parent=Inflaton}
{title2=$V(\phi)$}

The <inflaton potential> is the <potential energy> density of an <inflaton> as a function of its field value. Its derivative enters the <inflaton equation of motion>, while its slope and curvature enter the <potential slow-roll parameters>.

= Vacuum domination by a scalar field
{parent=Inflaton}
{title2=$\dot\phi^2/2\ll V(\phi)$}

A homogeneous canonical <scalar field> with positive <scalar potential> has <energy density> $\rho_\phi=\dot\phi^2/2+V$ and <pressure> $P_\phi=\dot\phi^2/2-V$. When its <potential energy> dominates its <kinetic energy>, the <equation-of-state parameter> approaches $-1$. If other components and spatial curvature are negligible, the <Friedmann acceleration equation> gives <accelerating expansion>. Small initial <kinetic energy> is not enough to ensure long <cosmic inflation>; the field must also remain on a sufficiently flat part of the <scalar potential>, as measured by the <potential slow-roll parameters>.

= Inflaton equation of motion
{parent=Inflaton}

For a homogeneous canonical inflaton in an expanding <FLRW metric>,
$$
\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0.
$$
The expansion contributes the damping term called <Hubble friction>.

= Frozen scalar fields and the Friedmann constraint
{parent=Inflaton equation of motion}

For a homogeneous canonical <scalar field>, the <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation> reduces to $\dot\phi(\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V')=0$. It implies the <inflaton equation of motion> wherever $\dot\phi\ne0$, and by continuity at isolated turning points. It cannot by itself exclude a frozen field on a time interval. Indeed, choose $\phi=\phi_0$ with $V(\phi_0)>0$ but $V'(\phi_0)\ne0$; a <de Sitter scale factor in flat slicing> with $H^2=V(\phi_0)/(3M_{\rm Pl}^2)$ satisfies the homogeneous <Friedmann equation> and <Friedmann acceleration equation>, but not the scalar equation. Varying the <inflaton action in an expanding universe> supplies the missing equation also for a frozen field, requiring $V'(\phi_0)=0$.

= Exact hyperbolic-sine scalar-field cosmology
{parent=Inflaton}

In a flat universe containing a homogeneous canonical <scalar field>, the ansatz
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_0\log\tanh(\lambda t)
$$
has the exact solution
$$
H=\lambda\frac{\phi_0^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}\coth(2\lambda t),
\qquad
a=a_0[\sinh(2\lambda t)]^{\phi_0^2/(2M_{\rm Pl}^2)}.
$$
It interpolates between decelerating power-law expansion at early times and exponential accelerated expansion at late times when $2/3<\phi_0^2/M_{\rm Pl}^2<2$.

= Inflaton action in an expanding universe
{parent=Inflaton}

For a canonical scalar field in a spatially flat expanding universe,
$$
S[\phi]=\int d^3x\,dt\,a^3
\left[
\frac12\dot\phi^2
-\frac{c^2}{2a^2}|\nabla\phi|^2
-V(\phi)
\right].
$$
The field <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi-\frac{c^2}{a^2}\nabla^2\phi+V'(\phi)=0.
$$

= Inflationary scalar Fourier mode
{parent=Inflaton action in an expanding universe}

For nearly constant inflaton potential and constant $H_{\rm inf}$, the spatial <Fourier transform> obeys
$$
\ddot{\widehat\phi}_{\mathbf k}
+3H_{\rm inf}\dot{\widehat\phi}_{\mathbf k}
+\frac{c^2k^2}{a^2}\widehat\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$

= Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode
{c}
{parent=Inflationary scalar Fourier mode}

For $a=-1/(H_{\rm inf}\tau)$, the rescaled mode
$$
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}=a\widehat\phi_{\mathbf k}
$$
satisfies
$$
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}''
+\left(c^2k^2-\frac2{\tau^2}\right)
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$

= Conformal-time quadratic action for an inflaton perturbation
{title2=$S^{(2)}=\tfrac12\int d\tau\,d^3x\{(f')^2-(\nabla f)^2+(a''/a-a^2V'')f^2\}$}
{parent=Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode}

Neglecting metric perturbations, the rescaling $f=a\delta\phi$ removes the expansion friction from the scalar perturbation action after a boundary term is discarded. Its canonical momentum is $f'$, and each real Fourier oscillator has time-dependent squared frequency $k^2+a^2V''-a''/a$. The oscillator can become inverted on <superhorizon scales>; the canonical commutation relations still apply.

= Bunch-Davies vacuum
{c}
{parent=Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode}
{wiki=Bunch–Davies_vacuum}

The Bunch-Davies vacuum selects the mode that approaches the positive-frequency ground-state mode of angular frequency $ck$ in the far past $\tau\to-\infty$.

= Bunch-Davies mode normalization in a finite comoving volume
{c}
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}
{title2=$f_k=H(-\tau+i/k)e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{2k\mathcal V},\quad a^2\mathcal V(f_kf_k^{*\prime}-f_k^*f_k')=i$}

For a <minimally coupled massless scalar field> in flat <de Sitter spacetime>, $a=-1/(H\tau)$ and the <canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode> is $u_k=af_k$. The equation $u_k''+(k^2-2/\tau^2)u_k=0$ and the positive-frequency condition $u_k\sim e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{2k\mathcal V}$ select the displayed mode. Direct differentiation verifies its <Wronskian> normalization, which ensures the equal-time <canonical commutation relations> with box-normalized creation and annihilation operators. A conformally coupled massless scalar has a different mode equation.

= Cosmological horizon exit
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}

A Fourier mode exits the Hubble horizon during inflation when $k=aH$. On superhorizon scales $k\ll aH$, the growing mode of a sufficiently light scalar perturbation approaches a constant.

= Superhorizon scale
{parent=Cosmological horizon exit}

= Super-Hubble scale
{synonym}

A cosmological mode is on a superhorizon scale when its physical wavenumber is smaller than the Hubble expansion rate, $k/a\ll H$, equivalently $k\ll aH$.

= Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}

A Fourier-space variance $P_k$ in three dimensions is scale invariant when the dimensionless power per logarithmic wavenumber interval,
$$
\Delta^2(k)=\frac{k^3P_k}{2\pi^2},
$$
is independent of $k$. For a massless inflaton mode at late times,
$$
P_k\longrightarrow\frac{\hbar H_{\rm inf}^2}{2c^3k^3},
$$
so $\Delta^2(k)$ is constant.

= Infrared qualification of a scale-invariant covariance
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}
{title2=$C(r)-C(r_0)=-\frac{A}{2\pi^2}\log(r/r_0)$}

An exact $P(k)=A/k^3$ has divergent unsubtracted point <variance>. Its finite isotropic <covariance> difference is the displayed logarithm, unchanged by simultaneous dilation of $r,r_0$. Thus the nonzero-mode scaling statement has a natural subtraction interpretation. Exact dilation invariance of a continuous finite-variance isotropic <covariance> itself would instead force a constant <covariance>, corresponding only to a zero-mode spectral measure. Cutoffs or a zero-mode subtraction must accompany the nontrivial spectrum.

= Equal-time two-point function of a de Sitter scalar
{title2=$P_{\delta\phi}(k,\tau)=H^2(1+k^2\tau^2)/(2k^3)$}
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}

For a light scalar in the <Bunch-Davies vacuum> with metric perturbations neglected, the mode $e^{-ik\tau}(1-i/(k\tau))/\sqrt{2k}$ gives the displayed equal-time spectrum. The dimensionless power tends to $H^2/(4\pi^2)$ on <superhorizon scales>. The exact massless infinite-volume correlation has an infrared divergence; a finite-volume or finite-duration infrared regulator, and smearing for coincident products, makes the correlators well defined.

= Frozen contribution to a de Sitter scalar spectrum
{parent=Equal-time two-point function of a de Sitter scalar}
{title2=$P_{\rm frozen}=H^2/(2k^3),\quad\Delta^2_{\rm frozen}=H^2/(4\pi^2)$}

= Frozen scalar power
{synonym}

The second term in the <equal-time two-point function of a de Sitter scalar> is independent of time for constant $H$. It dominates on <superhorizon scales> and has constant dimensionless <power spectrum>, giving a <scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum>. The <infrared qualification of a scale-invariant covariance> still applies to the formally infinite-volume coincident variance.

= Subhorizon vacuum contribution to a de Sitter scalar spectrum
{parent=Equal-time two-point function of a de Sitter scalar}
{title2=$\Delta^2_{\rm vac}(k,\tau)=k^2/(4\pi^2a^2)$}

= Subhorizon vacuum contribution
{synonym}

The <equal-time two-point function of a de Sitter scalar> has a contribution $P_{\rm vac}=1/(2ka^2)$, giving the displayed dimensionless <power spectrum> per logarithmic <wavenumber>. At fixed comoving <wavenumber> it decreases as $a^{-2}$; on short physical scales it reproduces the vacuum fluctuations of a <massless scalar field> in <Minkowski spacetime>. Its coincident integral is ultraviolet divergent, so the fixed-mode decrease cannot be interchanged with an unregulated integral over all modes.

= Primordial scalar amplitude
{title2=$A_s$}
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}

At leading slow-roll order, the dimensionless primordial curvature-perturbation amplitude at horizon exit is
$$
A_s=\frac{H^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon_VM_{\rm Pl}^2}
=\frac{V}{24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4\epsilon_V}.
$$

= Scalar spectral index
{title2=$n_s$}
{parent=Primordial scalar amplitude}
{wiki}

= Spectral tilt
{synonym}

The scalar spectral index describes the departure of the primordial scalar spectrum from scale invariance. To first order in potential slow-roll parameters,
$$
n_s-1\simeq-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V.
$$

= Running of the scalar spectral index
{parent=Scalar spectral index}
{title2=$\alpha_s=dn_s/d\log k\simeq16\epsilon_V\eta_V-24\epsilon_V^2-2\xi_V^2$}

The running describes the scale dependence of the <scalar spectral index>. Differentiating $n_s-1\simeq-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V$ with the <potential slow-roll flow equations> gives the displayed second-order slow-roll result. It includes the <third potential slow-roll parameter> and is generally smaller than the leading spectral tilt.

= Scalar spectral index in potential slow-roll parameters
{parent=Scalar spectral index}
{title2=$n_s\simeq1-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V$}

The leading scalar spectrum is proportional to $V^3/V'^2$. Along a slow-roll trajectory, $d\phi/d\ln a\simeq-M_{\rm pl}^2V'/V$ and $d\ln k/d\ln a=1-\epsilon_H$. Differentiating at horizon exit and keeping first order gives $n_s-1=-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V$. The derivative-factor correction contributes at second order.

= Slow-roll approximation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

= Slow-roll
{synonym}

= Slow-roll inflation
{synonym}

= Slow-roll equations
{synonym}

For a canonical scalar field with potential $V(\phi)$, slow roll reduces the field and Friedmann equations to
$$
H^2\simeq\frac{V}{3M_{\rm Pl}^2},
\qquad
3H\dot\phi\simeq-V'.
$$

= Relaxation to the slow-roll attractor
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{title2=$\dot\phi=-\frac{V'}{3H}(1-e^{-3H\Delta t})$}

With approximately constant positive $H$ and potential slope $V'$, a homogeneous <scalar field> initially at rest solves $\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi=-V'$ with the displayed <velocity>. The transient decays on time $1/(3H)$, leaving the <slow-roll approximation> $3H\dot\phi\simeq-V'$. The fractional potential change over a <Hubble time> is of order $M_{\rm Pl}^2(V'/V)^2$. Sustained slow roll normally uses both a small slope parameter and a small absolute curvature parameter $|M_{\rm Pl}^2V''/V|$. Local derivative bounds must hold over the traversed field interval; initial small <kinetic energy> alone is not a global inflation-duration theorem.

= Horizon-exit logarithmic derivative in slow roll
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{title2=$\frac d{d\log k}=\frac1{1-\epsilon_H}\frac{\dot\phi}H\frac d{d\phi}\simeq-\frac{V'}V\frac d{d\phi}$}

Along <cosmological horizon exit> $k\propto aH$, differentiation gives $d\log k/d\log a=1-\epsilon_H$, where $\epsilon_H=-\dot H/H^2$. The <chain rule> gives the first expression. In <reduced Planck units>, the <slow-roll equations> $3H\dot\phi\simeq-V'$ and $3H^2\simeq V$ give the second expression to leading order. Applied to a quantity already of slow-roll order, the omitted factor changes the answer only at second order.

= Single-field slow-roll inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

Single-field slow-roll inflation uses one canonical scalar clock whose potential evolves slowly enough for the slow-roll parameters to remain small. Its adiabatic long mode produces the <single-field inflation squeezed-limit consistency relation>.

= Starobinsky inflation
{c}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Starobinsky inflation is an inflationary model whose Einstein-frame scalar potential can be written
$$
V(\phi)=V_0\left(1-e^{-\sqrt{2/3}\,\phi/M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^2.
$$
The exponentially flat large-field plateau supports <slow-roll inflation>, while the potential is quadratic near its minimum.

= Hubble slow-roll parameter
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A Hubble slow-roll parameter measures the fractional change of the Hubble rate or of a preceding slow-roll parameter per e-fold. Small values make the inflationary background evolve adiabatically.

= Potential and Hubble slow-roll parameter relation
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}
{title2=$\eta\simeq4\epsilon_V-2\eta_V$}

For a canonical <inflaton> under the <slow-roll approximation>, $\dot\phi/H\simeq-M_{\rm Pl}^2V'/V$ and $\epsilon\simeq\epsilon_V=(M_{\rm Pl}^2/2)(V'/V)^2$. Differentiating $\log\epsilon_V$ along this trajectory gives
$$
\eta\simeq2\left(\frac{V''}{V'}-\frac{V'}V\right)\frac{\dot\phi}{H}=4\epsilon_V-2\eta_V.
$$
Here $\eta=d\log\epsilon/d\log a$, not the alternate convention $-\ddot\phi/(H\dot\phi)$. The logarithmic definition requires $\epsilon>0$; exact de Sitter expansion is treated as a limit.

= First Hubble slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}

The first Hubble slow-roll parameter is
$$
\epsilon=-\frac{\dot H}{H^2}.
$$
For a canonical inflaton, $\epsilon=\dot\phi^2/(2M_{\rm Pl}^2H^2)\geq0$.

= Second Hubble slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\eta$}
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}

Using the e-fold variable $N=\log a$, define
$$
\eta=\frac{d\log\epsilon}{dN}.
$$

= Comoving curvature perturbation
{title2=$\mathcal R$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The comoving curvature perturbation is the scalar curvature perturbation on hypersurfaces orthogonal to the total matter flow. For single-field adiabatic inflation it is conserved on superhorizon scales.

= Gradient evolution identity for comoving curvature
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$-A\mathcal R'=\mathcal Hc_a^2\nabla^2\phi$}

For adiabatic scalar perturbations without anisotropic stress, define $A=4\pi Ga^2(\bar\rho+\bar P)=\mathcal H^2-\mathcal H'$ and $c_a^2=\bar P'/\bar\rho'$. In <conformal Newtonian gauge>, $\mathcal R=-\phi-\mathcal H(\phi'+\mathcal H\phi)/A$. Background conservation gives $A'/A=-\mathcal H(1+3c_a^2)$. Differentiating the expression for $\mathcal R$ and using the scalar-potential equation gives the displayed identity. For regular adiabatic modes with nonzero background enthalpy, evolution is suppressed on scales $k\ll\mathcal H$.

= Mukhanov-Sasaki variable
{c}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$v=z\mathcal R$}

= Canonical curvature perturbation
{synonym}

For single-field adiabatic inflation, the Mukhanov-Sasaki variable rescales the <comoving curvature perturbation> by $z=a\sqrt{2\epsilon}M_{\rm Pl}$, where $M_{\rm Pl}$ is the <reduced Planck mass>. Its quadratic bulk <action> is $\tfrac12\int[(v')^2-(\nabla v)^2+(z''/z)v^2]$, after a boundary term is removed. A <Fourier transform> mode obeys $v_k''+(k^2-z''/z)v_k=0$ and has canonical <Wronskian normalization>. The mass scale and sound-speed factors depend on the underlying scalar theory; this definition assumes the canonical single-field action and positive slow-roll kinetic coefficient.

= Superhorizon conservation of single-field comoving curvature
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$\dot{\mathcal R}\simeq0\quad(k\ll aH)$}

For an adiabatic single-field attractor, gradient terms are negligible well outside the <Hubble radius> and the growing comoving-curvature mode is constant. The separate-universe interpretation identifies it with the difference of local expansion histories. The conservation statement can fail for a non-attractor phase or an independent entropy mode.

= Inflaton clock-shift origin of curvature perturbations
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$\mathcal R=H\delta\phi_{\rm flat}/\dot\phi$}

On a single-field attractor, a field fluctuation changes the local point along the background trajectory. The corresponding time shift has magnitude $|\delta\phi/\dot\phi|$, and local expansion converts it into curvature of magnitude $H|\delta\phi/\dot\phi|$. On spatially flat slices a standard sign convention gives $\mathcal R=H\delta\phi_{\rm flat}/\dot\phi$. The vacuum mode amplitude $H/(2\pi)$ then determines the scalar curvature spectrum.

= De Sitter curvature mode function
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{c}
{title2=$u_k(\tau)$}

For approximately constant $H$ and $\epsilon$, a vacuum-normalized curvature mode is $u_k(\tau)=H(1+ik\tau)e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2k^3}$. Its derivative is $u_k^\prime=Hk^2\tau e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2k^3}$. Ordered unequal-time <Wick contractions> use the complex conjugate at the earlier vertex, consistently with the <vacuum prescription for inflationary in-in integrals>.

= Non-adiabatic pressure perturbation
{title2=$\delta P_{\rm nad}$}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}

The non-adiabatic pressure perturbation is
$$
\delta P_{\rm nad}=\delta P-\frac{\dot{\bar P}}{\dot{\bar\rho}}\delta\rho.
$$
On <superhorizon scales> it sources the evolution of the <comoving curvature perturbation> through a convention-dependent signed relation proportional to $H\delta P_{\rm nad}/(\bar\rho+\bar P)$.

= Cosmological entropy perturbation
{parent=Non-adiabatic pressure perturbation}

= Isocurvature perturbation
{synonym}

A cosmological entropy perturbation changes the relative composition of cosmic fluids while leaving the total curvature initially unchanged. For <cold dark matter> and photons, a common relative entropy variable is
$$
S_{c\gamma}=\delta_c-\frac34\delta_\gamma.
$$

= Slow-roll curvature power spectrum
{title2=$\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2$}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}

At leading order in canonical single-field slow roll and evaluated at horizon exit $k=aH$,
$$
\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2(k)=\frac1{2\epsilon M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left(\frac{H}{2\pi}\right)^2.
$$

= Inflationary potential normalization and matter power
{parent=Slow-roll curvature power spectrum}
{title2=$V\mapsto A V\ \Longrightarrow\ P_m\mapsto A P_m$}

At a fixed horizon-exit e-fold count and with the later cosmology fixed, rescaling a canonical single-field inflationary potential by a positive constant leaves its potential slow-roll parameters and field solution unchanged. The <slow-roll curvature power spectrum> is proportional to $V/\epsilon_V$, so the <matter power spectrum> scales by the same factor while its leading tilt and transfer-function shape remain unchanged. A changed specified reheating history can also shift the horizon-exit count assigned to a fixed physical wavenumber.

= Scalar spectral index in Hubble slow-roll parameters
{title2=$n_s-1=-2\epsilon-\eta$}
{parent=Slow-roll curvature power spectrum}

Differentiating the curvature spectrum at horizon exit gives
$$
n_s-1=\frac{d\log\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2}{d\log k}
=-2\epsilon-\eta
$$
to first order in Hubble slow-roll parameters.

= Primordial tensor power spectrum
{title2=$\Delta_t^2$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For the two graviton polarizations in canonical single-field inflation,
$$
\Delta_t^2(k)=\frac8{M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left(\frac{H}{2\pi}\right)^2
$$
at horizon exit.

= Graviton power spectrum
{title2=$P_\gamma(k)$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}

The graviton power spectrum is the two-point spectrum of a tensor metric perturbation. A convention may quote it per polarization or summed over both polarizations, so that choice must accompany its normalization.

= Adiabatic tensor mode
{title2=$\gamma_{ij}^{L}$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}

An adiabatic tensor mode is a long-wavelength transverse-traceless metric perturbation whose leading local effect can be produced by a <large spatial diffeomorphism>. Its effect on short modes is therefore constrained by a Ward identity.

= Cosmological soft-graviton consistency relation
{parent=Adiabatic tensor mode}

For a single adiabatic long tensor mode, the squeezed scalar-scalar-graviton bispectrum is the anisotropic momentum response of the scalar power spectrum:
$$
\lim_{q\to0}\frac{\langle\gamma^s_{\mathbf q}\varphi_{\mathbf k}
\varphi_{-\mathbf k-\mathbf q}\rangle'}{P_\gamma(q)}
=-\frac12\epsilon_{ij}^s k_i\partial_{k_j}P_\varphi(k).
$$

= Tensor spectral index
{title2=$n_T$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}
{wiki}

The tensor spectral index is the logarithmic slope $n_T=d\log\Delta_t^2/d\log k$. Canonical slow-roll inflation gives $n_T=-2\epsilon$ to first order.

= Tensor-to-scalar ratio
{title2=$r$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}
{wiki}

The tensor-to-scalar ratio is $r=\Delta_t^2/\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2$. Canonical single-field slow roll gives $r=16\epsilon$.

= Single-field inflation consistency relation
{title2=$r=-8n_T$}
{parent=Tensor-to-scalar ratio}

Combining $r=16\epsilon$ with $n_T=-2\epsilon$ gives the leading-order consistency relation
$$
r=-8n_T.
$$

= Blue primordial spectrum
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A primordial power spectrum is blue when its dimensionless power increases with wavenumber. Canonical single-field slow roll cannot produce a blue tensor spectrum because $\epsilon\geq0$ implies $n_T\leq0$, but its scalar spectrum can be blue when $\eta<-2\epsilon$.

= Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For $V(\phi)=m^2\phi^2$ in units where $3H^2\simeq V$ and for positive $\phi$,
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_i-\frac{2m}{\sqrt3}t,
$$
and
$$
a(t)=a_i\exp\left(
\frac{m\phi_i}{\sqrt3}t-\frac{m^2t^2}{3}
\right)
=a_i\exp\left(\frac{\phi_i^2-\phi(t)^2}{4}\right).
$$

= Quadratic-inflation expansion from Planck density
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$N\simeq4\pi(m_P/m)^2$}

For $V=m^2\phi^2/2$ and unreduced <Planck mass>, the <slow-roll e-fold count> is $N=2\pi(\phi_i^2-\phi_f^2)/m_P^2$. A formal initial potential $V_i\sim m_P^4$ gives $\phi_i^2\sim2m_P^4/m^2$, hence the displayed large-field estimate and expansion factor $e^N$. The final-field contribution is order one in $N$. Without specifying $m/m_P$ there is no unique numerical expansion factor. The extrapolation to Planck density is a classical estimate, rather than a controlled treatment of quantum-gravity corrections.

= Quadratic-inflation endpoint estimate
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$\phi_{\rm end}\simeq m_P/\sqrt{4\pi}$}

For $V=m^2\phi^2/2$ and unreduced <Planck mass> $m_P=G^{-1/2}$, the <potential slow-roll parameter> is $\epsilon_V=m_P^2(V'/V)^2/(16\pi)=m_P^2/(4\pi\phi^2)$. Its conventional value one gives the displayed endpoint estimate. Exact accelerated expansion ends at $\epsilon_H=1$, or $\dot\phi^2=V$ for a canonical scalar alone. Substitution of slow-roll formulas into this exact condition gives a different order-one coefficient because slow roll is failing there; a precise endpoint requires integrating the full <inflaton equation of motion> and <Friedmann equation>. The large-field e-fold estimate is insensitive to this order-one endpoint uncertainty.

= Quadratic-inflation field interval from a wavenumber interval
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$\Delta\Phi/M_{\rm Pl}\simeq2\Delta\log k/\sqrt{4N+2}$}

For a quadratic <inflaton> potential ending at the leading estimate $\epsilon_V=1$, $\Phi/M_{\rm Pl}=\sqrt{4N+2}$. <Cosmological horizon exit> gives $d\log k/dN=-1+\epsilon_H$. A narrow logarithmic interval therefore probes a field span $2\Delta\log k/(\sqrt{4N+2}(1-\epsilon_H))$, to the accuracy of the <slow-roll approximation>. At sixty e-folds, five logarithmic wavenumber units probe about $0.64M_{\rm Pl}$ around a background field near $15.6M_{\rm Pl}$.

= Quadratic-inflation tilt-tensor relation
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$r=4(1-n_s)$}

The quadratic potential has equal <potential slow-roll parameters>, $\epsilon_V=\eta_V=1/(2N+1)$. Consequently $n_s=1-4/(2N+1)$ and $r=16/(2N+1)$ at leading order. The relation $r=4(1-n_s)$ is independent of the <inflaton> mass, so a satisfactory scalar tilt does not by itself guarantee a satisfactory tensor prediction.

= Quadratic-inflation amplitude calibration
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$m/M_{\rm pl}=\sqrt{24\pi^2A_s}/(2N+1)$}

For a quadratic potential with the end set by $\epsilon_V=1$, a mode leaving $N$ e-folds earlier has $\phi_*^2=(4N+2)M_{\rm pl}^2$. Its curvature amplitude is $(m/M_{\rm pl})^2(2N+1)^2/(24\pi^2)$. Thus the scalar amplitude fixes the <inflaton> mass while the tilt and tensor ratio depend only on the exit e-fold count at this order.

= Quartic-potential slow-roll solution
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{title2=$\phi=\phi_i e^{-M_P\sqrt{\lambda/(6\pi)}(t-t_i)}$}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4/4$ with $\lambda>0$, use <natural units> and the unreduced <Planck mass> $M_P=G^{-1/2}$. The <slow-roll equations> give $H\simeq\sqrt{2\pi\lambda/3}\,\phi^2/M_P$ and $\dot\phi\simeq-M_P\sqrt{\lambda/(6\pi)}\,\phi$. Integrating gives the displayed exponential field decay, valid on either sign branch, and
$$
\frac{a(t)}{a_i}\simeq\exp\left[\frac{\pi}{M_P^2}(\phi_i^2-\phi(t)^2)\right].
$$
The <potential slow-roll parameters> require $\phi^2\gg M_P^2$. The expression is valid only during <slow-roll inflation>; its formal late-time saturation does not describe the subsequent <reheating> or radiation era.

= Quartic-inflation expansion from Planck density
{parent=Quartic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$N\sim2\pi/\sqrt\lambda$}

With unreduced <Planck mass> $M_P$ and initial potential-dominated density $\lambda\phi_i^4/4\sim M_P^4$, one has $\phi_i^2\sim2M_P^2/\sqrt\lambda$. The <slow-roll e-fold count> is $N\simeq\pi(\phi_i^2-\phi_e^2)/M_P^2$. Thus small positive $\lambda$ produces a very large expansion factor $e^N$ in this classical quartic model. The order-one endpoint correction is negligible compared with $2\pi/\sqrt\lambda$; initial <kinetic energy>, spatial curvature and the model's validity at trans-Planckian field values are independent assumptions.

= Quartic-inflation endpoint estimate
{parent=Quartic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$|\phi_e|\simeq M_P/\sqrt\pi$}

For $V=\lambda\phi^4/4$ and unreduced <Planck mass> $M_P$, the <potential slow-roll parameter> is $\epsilon_V=M_P^2/(\pi\phi^2)$. Setting it to one gives the displayed conventional endpoint. The exact end of scalar-dominated <accelerating expansion> is $\epsilon_H=1$, equivalently $\dot\phi^2=V$. Since <slow roll> is failing near that point, a precise endpoint requires evolving the full <inflaton equation of motion>; potential and kinetic criteria evaluated with a failing approximation need not give the same order-one coefficient.

= Potential slow-roll parameter
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The potential slow-roll parameter is
$$
\epsilon_V=\frac{M_{\rm Pl}^2}{2}
\left(\frac{V'}V\right)^2.
$$
Under the slow-roll equations, $\dot\phi^2/(2V)\simeq\epsilon_V/3$.

= Potential slow-roll flow equations
{parent=Potential slow-roll parameter}
{title2=$d\epsilon_V/d\log k=4\epsilon_V^2-2\epsilon_V\eta_V,\quad d\eta_V/d\log k=2\epsilon_V\eta_V-\xi_V^2$}

At horizon exit during single-field <slow-roll inflation>, $d/d\log k\simeq-M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2(V'/V)d/d\Phi$. Applying this operator to the <potential slow-roll parameter> and the <second potential slow-roll parameter> yields the displayed leading-order flow equations. Corrections to the horizon-exit derivative contribute only at higher slow-roll order here.

= Third potential slow-roll parameter
{parent=Potential slow-roll parameter}
{title2=$\xi_V^2=M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^4 V'V'''/V^2$}

Despite its notation, $\xi_V^2$ need not be nonnegative. It is the signed dimensionless derivative combination entering the leading <running of the scalar spectral index>.

= Second potential slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\eta_V$}
{parent=Potential slow-roll parameter}

The second potential slow-roll parameter is
$$
\eta_V=M_{\rm Pl}^2\frac{V''}{V}.
$$
Slow roll usually requires both $\epsilon_V\ll1$ and $|\eta_V|\ll1$.

= Effective equation of state of an oscillating scalar field
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

Suppose a homogeneous scalar field oscillates much faster than the <Hubble parameter> in a monomial potential $V(\phi)=\lambda|\phi|^n$. The <virial theorem> averaged over one oscillation gives $\langle\dot\phi^2\rangle=n\langle V\rangle$, and hence
$$
\langle w\rangle=\frac{\langle P\rangle}{\langle\rho\rangle}=\frac{n-2}{n+2},
\qquad
\rho\propto a^{-6n/(n+2)}.
$$
A quadratic minimum therefore behaves like <pressureless matter>, whereas a quartic minimum behaves like <radiation in cosmology>.

= Hilltop inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Hilltop inflation begins near a local maximum of the inflaton potential. For
$$
V(\phi)=\Lambda^4-\frac12m^2\phi^2,
$$
a positive field rolls away from the maximum at $\phi=0$.

= Quadratic hilltop slow-roll prediction
{parent=Hilltop inflation}

For $V(\phi)=V_0-m^2\phi^2/2$ with $V_0$ dominant,
$$
\eta_V\simeq-\frac{m^2M_{\rm Pl}^2}{V_0},
\qquad
\epsilon_V\simeq\frac12\eta_V^2\frac{\phi^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}.
$$
If $\widetilde N$ e-folds remain and $n_s-1\simeq2\eta_V$, then
$$
\phi=\phi_e e^{(n_s-1)\widetilde N/2},
\qquad
r\simeq2(n_s-1)^2\left(\frac{\phi_e}{M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^2e^{(n_s-1)\widetilde N}.
$$

= Monomial inflation potential
{title2=$V(\phi)=\lambda M_{\rm Pl}^4(\phi/M_{\rm Pl})^\alpha$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A monomial inflation potential is a positive power of the inflaton. Its potential slow-roll parameter is $\epsilon_V=\alpha^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/(2\phi^2)$.

= Monomial-inflation curvature amplitude
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}
{title2=$\mathcal P_{\mathcal R}=V/(24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4\epsilon_V)$}

For $V=C_p(\Phi/M_{\rm Pl})^p$ and the leading endpoint estimate $\epsilon_V=1$, the <slow-roll approximation> gives $\Phi_N^2/M_{\rm Pl}^2=2pN+p^2/2$ and $\epsilon_V=p/(4N+p)$. Thus $\mathcal P_{\mathcal R}=C_p(2pN+p^2/2)^{p/2}(4N+p)/(24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4p)$ and the <scalar spectral index> obeys $n_s-1=-2(p+2)/(4N+p)$. The coefficient sets the amplitude, whereas the monomial power and exit e-fold count set the leading tilt. Changing the power without specifying the coefficient leaves the overall normalization undetermined.

= Monomial inflation with a prescribed endpoint
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}
{title2=$\phi_N^2=\phi_e^2+2nNM_{\rm Pl}^2$}

For $V=\lambda\phi^n$ on $\phi>0$, <slow-roll approximation> gives $\epsilon\simeq n^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/(2\phi^2)$ and $\eta\simeq2nM_{\rm Pl}^2/\phi^2$. The <slow-roll e-fold count> integrates to $N\simeq(\phi_N^2-\phi_e^2)/(2nM_{\rm Pl}^2)$. For $n=2,3,4$, the loose order-one conditions $\epsilon\leq1$ and $|\eta|\leq1$ imply $\phi^2\geq2nM_{\rm Pl}^2$ in this approximation. Genuine slow roll requires a much stronger hierarchy. A prescribed endpoint $\phi_e^2=2nM_{\rm Pl}^2$ gives $\phi_N^2=2n(N+1)M_{\rm Pl}^2$ and
$$
n_s\simeq1-\frac{n+2}{2(N+1)},\qquad r\simeq\frac{4n}{N+1}.
$$
The actual end criterion for accelerated expansion is $\epsilon=1$, whose leading potential estimate instead gives $\phi_e^2\simeq n^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/2$. The prescribed endpoint is a convention, not an exact slow-roll or inflation boundary.

= Quartic-inflation tilt-tensor relation
{title2=$n_s-1=-3r/16$}
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}

For canonical slow-roll inflation with $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4$, the leading <scalar spectral index> and <tensor-to-scalar ratio> obey
$$
n_s-1=-\frac3{16}r.
$$
It is a parameter-free consistency check of the quartic potential at leading slow-roll order.

= Slow-roll e-fold count
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The number of e-folds between field values $\phi_i$ and $\phi_f$ is
$$
N\simeq\frac1{\sqrt2M_{\rm Pl}}
\int_{\phi_f}^{\phi_i}\frac{d\phi}{\sqrt{\epsilon_V(\phi)}}
$$
when the field rolls monotonically down the potential.

= Monomial slow-roll e-fold count
{parent=Slow-roll e-fold count}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^n/n$ under the stated Cambridge Tripos normalization,
$$
N_e=\frac{4\pi G}{c^4n}
(\phi_i^2-\phi_f^2).
$$
If slow roll ends by kinetic-potential equality, then
$$
\phi_f^2\simeq\frac{n^2c^4}{48\pi G},
\qquad
N_e\simeq\frac n{12}
\left[\left(\frac{\phi_i}{\phi_f}\right)^2-1\right].
$$

= Natural inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Natural inflation uses a periodic potential
$$
V(\phi)=V_0[1+\cos(\phi/f)].
$$
Its potential slow-roll parameter obeys
$$
\sqrt{\epsilon_V}=\frac{M_{\rm Pl}}{\sqrt2f}
\tan\frac{\phi}{2f}
$$
on the branch where the tangent is positive.

= E-fold count for the natural-inflation cosine potential
{parent=Natural inflation}

For the cosine potential,
$$
N=\frac{2f^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left[
\log\sin\frac{\phi_i}{2f}
-\log\sin\frac{\phi_f}{2f}
\right].
$$

= Ultra-slow-roll inflation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

Ultra-slow-roll inflation occurs on an exceptionally flat potential. The homogeneous field velocity decays as $\dot\phi\propto a^{-3}$, so $\epsilon\propto a^{-6}$ and the second Hubble slow-roll parameter approaches $-6$.

= Growing curvature perturbation in ultra-slow-roll inflation
{parent=Ultra-slow-roll inflation}

In ultra-slow-roll inflation, $z=a\sqrt{2\epsilon}\propto a^{-2}\propto\eta^2$. The comoving curvature mode equation is
$$
\mathcal R_k''+\frac4\eta\mathcal R_k'+k^2\mathcal R_k=0,
$$
and its Bunch-Davies mode is proportional to $(1+ik\eta)e^{-ik\eta}/\eta^3$. Unlike ordinary slow roll, the superhorizon curvature perturbation grows as $a^3$.

= Classicality parameter of a cosmological perturbation
{parent=Growing curvature perturbation in ultra-slow-roll inflation}

For a perturbation $X$, a normalized commutator
$$
\mathcal C=
\frac{|\langle[X,\dot X]\rangle|}
{\sqrt{\langle XX\rangle\langle\dot X\dot X\rangle}}
$$
compares its quantum commutator with its root-mean-square phase-space fluctuations. A limit $\mathcal C\to0$ signals effective classicality.

= Curvaton
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

A curvaton is a light spectator scalar field whose energy density is negligible during inflation but whose fluctuations can later generate the primordial curvature perturbation.

= Curvaton mechanism
{parent=Curvaton}
{wiki}

In the curvaton mechanism, the curvaton's relative energy density grows after inflation and its initially isocurvature fluctuation becomes an adiabatic curvature fluctuation when the curvaton decays.
